const intro={"n":3,"title":"Two Years Apart: Fierro and Cruz Survive Captivity","nums":[461,463,470],"summary":"Fierro tells how he and Cruz endure confinement after arriving beyond the frontier. Their captors initially keep them separated under surveillance; Fierro says this lasts about two years. A leader eventually allows them to live together. They make a simple shelter from horsehide near tall grass and try to survive by hunting animals for food. The hardships are presented from Fierro's perspective, which repeatedly adds prejudiced generalizations about Indigenous people.","people":"Martín Fierro as the first-person narrator; Sergeant Cruz; an Indigenous leader and guards seen only through Fierro's account.","themes":"Captivity and separation; endurance and friendship; practical survival skills; distinctions between witnessed events and racialized claims.","words":"cacique (Indigenous leader); toldo (shelter); pajal (tall grass); bagual (untamed horse); piche (armadillo); cautiverio (captivity).","questions":["How long does Fierro say he and Cruz were kept apart?","What changes when the leader allows them to live together?","How do hunting and shelter-building become necessary for survival?","Which assertions are narrated experiences, and which are prejudiced stereotypes?","Why is the bond between Fierro and Cruz especially significant in this canto?"],"analysis":"There is a narratively verifiable sequence: separation, surveillance, permission to reunite, a makeshift shelter and hunting for sustenance. Fierro also claims that Indigenous people are uniformly cruel or ignorant, which this text cannot establish as historical fact. A good student analysis distinguishes the events inside the story from that blanket characterization.","related":"/en/magazine/indigenous-peoples-frontier/","relatedTitle":"Indigenous peoples and frontier history"};
